# Econ Lab, part C: global trade; doing business around the world.
# Run by build_data.py in the same namespace as econ_a.py, so p() and P already exist.

# ======================= Global trade =======================
p("EC:016", "The nature of global trade",
 ["Global trade is the exchange of goods and services across national borders. Exports are goods and services sold to other countries; imports are those bought from other countries. Countries trade because of specialization: absolute advantage means producing something with fewer resources than another country, and comparative advantage means producing it at a lower opportunity cost. Even if one country is better at everything, both gain by specializing in what they give up least to make, then trading.",
  "The balance of trade is exports minus imports. A trade deficit means a country imports more than it exports; a surplus means the opposite. Governments shape trade with barriers: tariffs (taxes on imports), quotas (limits on quantity), embargoes (bans on trade with a country) and subsidies to domestic producers. Free trade removes barriers; protectionism uses them to shield domestic industries, often raising prices for consumers and inviting retaliation."],
 ["Exports out, imports in.",
  "Comparative advantage: specialize in the lowest opportunity cost, then trade.",
  "Balance of trade = exports - imports (deficit or surplus).",
  "Barriers: tariffs, quotas, embargoes, subsidies.",
  "Free trade vs protectionism."],
 ["Banks finance trade with letters of credit, which guarantee the exporter gets paid once shipping documents are presented.",
  "Tariffs raise costs for importers, which affects their profits and ability to repay loans.",
  "Currency exchange and international payments are financial services that make trade possible."],
 ["The U.S. trade deficit in goods and services was $105.0 billion in September 2026; exports were $321.5 billion and imports a record $426.6 billion (U.S. Census Bureau and BEA, Nov. 4, 2026).",
  "On Feb. 20, 2026 the Supreme Court ruled 6-3 in Learning Resources v. Trump that the International Emergency Economic Powers Act does not authorize tariffs; tariffs under Section 232 and Section 301 of other trade laws remained (Supreme Court; Congressional Research Service).",
  "The average effective U.S. tariff rate was about 6.7% in July 2026, with China facing the highest rate, about 22.8% (Penn Wharton Budget Model, Sept. 2026)."],
 "Don't mix up absolute and comparative advantage. Trade is based on comparative advantage: lower opportunity cost, not just being better at it. And a trade deficit isn't automatically 'losing money'; it means buying more abroad than selling, often paid for with foreign investment flowing in.",
 "\"Tariffs are a tax on imports, and the importer pays them first. For this shop, the tariff on its foreign parts raises costs, so our plan prices it in and finds a second supplier.\"",
 [("How do tariffs affect this importer's costs and its customers?",
   "A tariff is a tax on imports paid by the importer, so it raises this business's costs right away. The owner then has three choices: raise prices, accept lower margins or switch suppliers. Most businesses pass some of it to customers. U.S. tariff policy has also shifted a lot since the Supreme Court's February 2026 ruling, so costs are uncertain. We'd build the tariff into the price of the most affected products, compare a supplier in a lower-tariff country and keep extra cash on hand."),
  ("Is a trade deficit bad for the U.S.?",
   "Not by itself. A trade deficit means Americans buy more from abroad than they sell, which gives consumers more choices and lower prices. The U.S. deficit was $105 billion in September 2026. Foreign sellers often invest those dollars back into U.S. stocks, bonds and businesses. It can hurt specific industries that lose out to imports, though. So we'd judge it by what's behind it: strong U.S. spending is different from failing exporters.")],
 [("Comparative advantage","The ability to produce a good at a lower opportunity cost than another country; the basis for trade.","Even the better producer gains by specializing."),
  ("Balance of trade","Exports minus imports; negative is a deficit, positive a surplus.","U.S. deficit: $105.0 billion in Sept. 2026."),
  ("Tariff vs quota vs embargo","Tariff: tax on imports. Quota: limit on quantity. Embargo: ban on trade with a country.","All are trade barriers.")])

p("EC:109", "The impact of globalization on business",
 ["Globalization is the growing connection of the world's economies through trade, investment, technology, travel and communication. For businesses, it opens bigger markets, access to cheaper materials and labor, new ideas and global talent. Companies build global supply chains that source parts from many countries, and consumers get more choice and lower prices.",
  "Globalization also brings risks and costs. Competition is global, so a local firm may compete with rivals anywhere. Long supply chains can break, as they did during the COVID-19 pandemic. Some jobs move overseas (offshoring), which hurts certain workers and communities. Companies now balance efficiency with resilience by diversifying suppliers, moving production closer to home (nearshoring or reshoring) and keeping more inventory."],
 ["Globalization: interconnected trade, investment, technology and communication.",
  "Benefits: bigger markets, lower costs, more choice.",
  "Costs: global competition, supply-chain risk, job shifts.",
  "Offshoring, nearshoring and reshoring.",
  "Businesses balance efficiency against resilience."],
 ["Investors can diversify globally: U.S. companies are only about 60% of global stock market value.",
  "Global banks move money across borders for trade, payroll and investment.",
  "Supply-chain risk affects borrowers' ability to deliver products and repay loans."],
 ["Supply-chain shift: Apple said a majority of iPhones sold in the U.S. in the June 2025 quarter came from India, as it moved production away from China to reduce tariff and concentration risk (Apple earnings call, May 2025).",
  "Supply shocks: during 2021-22, a global shortage of computer chips cut new-car production and pushed up car prices worldwide.",
  "U.S. companies make up roughly 60% of global stock market value, so global funds cover the other 40% (MSCI All Country World Index weights)."],
 "Don't present globalization as only good or only bad. Strong answers name a benefit, a risk and how this client should manage the trade-off, such as a second supplier in another region.",
 "\"Globalization lets this business buy parts at lower cost overseas, but one supplier in one country is a big risk. Like Apple shifting iPhone production to India, we'd add a second source.\"",
 [("Should this business buy from overseas suppliers?",
   "Yes, if the savings are real after all costs, but not from just one source. Overseas suppliers can be much cheaper, but the full cost includes shipping, tariffs, longer lead times and the risk of delays. Apple, for example, moved much of its U.S. iPhone production to India to avoid relying on one country. So we'd compare the total landed cost, keep a domestic or nearby backup supplier and hold a few weeks of extra inventory, so a disruption doesn't stop sales."),
  ("How does globalization affect our client's investments?",
   "It gives our client a chance to diversify beyond the U.S. Since U.S. companies are only about 60% of world stock value, a U.S.-only portfolio misses a big part of the global economy. Many U.S. companies also earn a large share of sales abroad. International funds add currency risk, though. So we'd suggest a low-cost total international index fund as part of the portfolio, often 20% to 40% of stocks, so our client benefits from global growth without betting on one country.")],
 [("Globalization","The growing interconnection of economies through trade, investment, technology and communication.","Bigger markets, more competition."),
  ("Offshoring vs reshoring","Offshoring: moving work abroad. Reshoring: bringing it back home. Nearshoring: moving it to a nearby country.","Mexico is a nearshoring hub for U.S. firms."),
  ("Global supply chain","A network of suppliers in multiple countries that produce parts of a final product.","Efficient but exposed to disruptions.")])

p("EC:100", "Exchange rates and the domestic economy",
 ["An exchange rate is the price of one currency in terms of another, such as how many euros or yen one U.S. dollar buys. Most major currencies, including the dollar, float: their value is set by supply and demand in the foreign exchange market, the world's largest financial market. Some countries fix (peg) their currency to another.",
  "Exchange rates are driven by interest rates (higher rates attract foreign money, strengthening a currency), inflation (high inflation weakens a currency), economic growth, trade balances, government debt, political stability, central bank actions and speculation. A strong dollar makes imports and foreign travel cheaper for Americans but makes U.S. exports more expensive abroad and lowers the dollar value of profits that U.S. companies earn overseas. A weak dollar does the opposite: it helps exporters and tourism to the U.S. but raises import prices, which can add to inflation."],
 ["Exchange rate = price of one currency in another.",
  "Floating (market-set) vs fixed (pegged).",
  "Drivers: interest rates, inflation, growth, trade, stability, speculation.",
  "Strong dollar: cheaper imports, harder exports, lower foreign profits in dollars.",
  "Weak dollar: helps exporters, raises import prices."],
 ["International funds carry currency risk: if the dollar strengthens, foreign returns shrink when converted back.",
  "Banks offer currency exchange, international wires and hedging tools (forward contracts) for businesses.",
  "Higher U.S. rates tend to attract foreign capital and strengthen the dollar."],
 ["The foreign exchange market traded about $9.6 trillion a day in April 2025, with the U.S. dollar on one side of about 89% of trades (Bank for International Settlements Triennial Survey, 2025).",
  "In mid-November 2026, one U.S. dollar bought about 159 Japanese yen, near the 160 level where Japan has stepped in to support its currency before (Reuters, Nov. 17, 2026).",
  "The euro fell about 5% against the dollar from late August to mid-November 2026, as markets expected the Fed to keep rates higher (FXStreet; Trading Economics, Nov. 2026)."],
 "Don't say a strong dollar is always good for the U.S. It helps importers and travelers but hurts exporters and U.S. companies' foreign earnings. Always say who wins and who loses.",
 "\"The dollar is strong right now, about 159 yen to the dollar. That makes this company's products more expensive for Japanese buyers, so we'd consider pricing in yen and using a forward contract to lock in the rate.\"",
 [("How would a stronger dollar affect this company that sells overseas?",
   "It would make the company's products more expensive for foreign buyers, and it shrinks the dollar value of every sale made in a foreign currency. If the euro falls 5% against the dollar, as it did from August to November 2026, a European customer's same euro payment converts into fewer dollars. That squeezes sales and margins. We'd recommend a forward contract to lock in exchange rates on big orders and look for growth in markets where the currency is steadier."),
  ("Should our client worry about currency risk in an international fund?",
   "A little, but it's usually a reason to diversify, not to avoid international investing. When the dollar strengthens, foreign returns shrink when converted to dollars; when it weakens, they grow. Over long periods those swings tend to even out. For a long-term investor, a broad international index fund for part of the stock portfolio still adds diversification. If our client wants less currency risk, some funds are currency-hedged, at a small extra cost.")],
 [("Exchange rate","The price of one currency expressed in another currency.","About 159 yen per dollar, Nov. 2026."),
  ("Floating vs fixed exchange rate","Floating: set by supply and demand. Fixed (pegged): tied to another currency by the government.","The dollar floats."),
  ("Strong dollar effects","Cheaper imports and foreign travel; more expensive exports; lower dollar value of foreign profits.","Hurts U.S. exporters.")])

p("EC:112", "Major trade alliances",
 ["A trade alliance (or agreement) is a deal between countries to reduce trade barriers among members. Types range from free-trade areas (members cut tariffs with each other but keep their own policies toward others), to customs unions (a shared tariff toward outsiders), to common markets and economic unions (free movement of goods, services, people and money, sometimes a shared currency).",
  "Major alliances include the United States-Mexico-Canada Agreement (USMCA), which replaced NAFTA in 2020; the European Union, a 27-country economic union with a single market, 21 of whose members use the euro since Bulgaria joined on Jan. 1, 2026; ASEAN, the Association of Southeast Asian Nations, which grew to 11 members when Timor-Leste joined in October 2025; Mercosur in South America; and the CPTPP, an 11-country Pacific deal plus the United Kingdom. The World Trade Organization (WTO) sets global trade rules for its 166 members and settles disputes. Alliances lower costs for members' businesses but can divert trade from outsiders and create uncertainty when terms change."],
 ["Trade agreements reduce barriers among members.",
  "Free-trade area < customs union < common market < economic union.",
  "USMCA (2020), EU (27 members; euro in 21), ASEAN (11), Mercosur, CPTPP.",
  "WTO sets global rules and settles disputes.",
  "Rules of origin decide which products qualify for lower tariffs."],
 ["Businesses that meet a trade deal's rules of origin can import or export with little or no tariff, which lowers costs and improves cash flow.",
  "Changes to trade deals add risk to businesses with cross-border supply chains, which lenders consider.",
  "Investors watch trade deals because they affect company earnings in exporting industries."],
 ["The U.S. declined to renew USMCA at its first joint review on July 1, 2026, which triggers annual reviews; the agreement remains in force and runs to 2036 unless extended (USTR; White & Case, July 2026).",
  "Bulgaria adopted the euro on Jan. 1, 2026, becoming the 21st euro-area country (European Central Bank).",
  "Timor-Leste became ASEAN's 11th member on Oct. 26, 2025, the bloc's first expansion since 1999 (ASEAN)."],
 "Don't call USMCA 'NAFTA.' NAFTA (1994) was replaced by USMCA in 2020. And don't say the July 2026 review ended USMCA; it's still in force, now with annual reviews.",
 "\"Because this business's parts come from Mexico, USMCA lets them cross the border tariff-free if they meet the rules of origin. Since the deal now faces annual reviews, we'd keep a backup plan.\"",
 [("How does USMCA help this business that buys parts from Mexico?",
   "It lets qualifying parts cross the border with little or no tariff, which keeps costs down. To qualify, the parts must meet USMCA's rules of origin, so the business needs documentation proving where they're made. Since the U.S. declined to renew the agreement at the July 2026 review, it now faces annual reviews, which adds uncertainty. So we'd confirm every part qualifies, keep the paperwork ready and price in a cushion in case terms change."),
  ("What happens to this business if a trade agreement changes?",
   "Its costs and supply chain could change quickly, so planning ahead matters. If tariffs return on goods that were tariff-free, the business pays more for imports or loses price competitiveness on exports. The USMCA review in July 2026 shows deals can change. We'd stress-test the budget for a 10% to 25% cost increase, line up an alternative supplier and avoid long fixed-price contracts with customers that can't adjust if tariffs rise.")],
 [("USMCA","United States-Mexico-Canada Agreement; replaced NAFTA in 2020.","Annual reviews since the July 2026 review."),
  ("European Union","A 27-country economic union with a single market; 21 members use the euro.","Bulgaria joined the euro on Jan. 1, 2026."),
  ("World Trade Organization (WTO)","The global body that sets trade rules and settles trade disputes among its 166 members.","Members commit to most-favored-nation treatment.")])

p("EC:113", "The political environment and world trade",
 ["A country's political environment, meaning its government, laws, stability and relations with other countries, shapes whether and how businesses trade there. Political risk is the chance that government action or instability will hurt a business. Sources include changes in leadership or policy, civil unrest or war, corruption, tariffs and trade barriers, sanctions and embargoes, currency controls that block moving money out of the country, and nationalization or expropriation (government taking over private businesses).",
  "Stable governments with clear laws and courts attract trade and investment. Unstable ones raise costs and risk. Businesses manage political risk by researching countries, diversifying across markets, partnering with local firms, buying political risk insurance (offered by agencies such as the U.S. International Development Finance Corporation and the World Bank's MIGA) and including exit plans in contracts."],
 ["Political risk: government action or instability that hurts business.",
  "Sources: policy changes, war, corruption, tariffs, sanctions, currency controls, expropriation.",
  "Stability and rule of law attract investment.",
  "Manage with research, diversification, local partners, political risk insurance.",
  "Elections and trade policy changes affect markets."],
 ["Sanctions can freeze financial assets and cut banks off from international payment networks.",
  "Investors in emerging markets demand higher returns to compensate for political risk.",
  "Lenders financing exports may require political risk insurance."],
 ["After Russia invaded Ukraine in 2022, Western governments imposed sanctions and removed several Russian banks from the SWIFT payment network, and about 1,000 companies curtailed or ended operations in Russia (EU Council; Yale Chief Executive Leadership Institute).",
  "McDonald's sold its 850 Russian restaurants and left the country in 2022 after more than 30 years there (McDonald's, 2022).",
  "U.S. trade policy shifted sharply in 2026: the Supreme Court struck down tariffs imposed under IEEPA in February, and the administration replaced them with tariffs under other trade laws (Supreme Court; Penn Wharton Budget Model, 2026)."],
 "Don't treat political risk as something only in faraway countries. Policy changes at home, like new tariffs or sanctions, are political risk too. And don't tell a client to avoid every risky country; the answer is usually to limit and diversify exposure.",
 "\"Political risk can wipe out an investment overnight, as investors in Russia learned in 2022. For this client, we'd keep any single-country exposure small and use a diversified fund.\"",
 [("How would you protect this business from political risk in a country it sells to?",
   "We'd research, diversify and insure. First, check the country's stability, laws and relations with the U.S. Second, avoid depending on that one market: build sales in at least two others. Third, get paid securely, using letters of credit or payment up front, and consider political risk insurance from the U.S. Development Finance Corporation or the World Bank's MIGA. When sanctions hit Russia in 2022, companies with diversified markets survived the loss far better than those that depended on it."),
  ("Should our client avoid investing in politically unstable countries?",
   "Not entirely, but our client should keep that exposure small and diversified. Politically risky markets can offer higher growth and returns, but they can also lose value fast. Investors in Russian stocks in 2022 couldn't sell when sanctions hit. A broad emerging-markets index fund spreads money across many countries, so one crisis doesn't sink the portfolio. We'd suggest a small slice, maybe 5% to 10% of stocks, only as part of a long-term plan.")],
 [("Political risk","The risk that government action or instability will hurt a business or investment.","War, sanctions, expropriation, currency controls."),
  ("Expropriation (nationalization)","A government taking ownership of private businesses or property in its country.","A major political risk for foreign investors."),
  ("Sanctions","Penalties, such as trade bans or asset freezes, that governments impose on another country.","Russian banks removed from SWIFT in 2022.")])

p("EC:104", "The global environment in which businesses operate",
 ["Businesses today operate in a global environment: they sell to customers abroad, buy from foreign suppliers, compete with international rivals, borrow and invest in global financial markets and follow rules set by international organizations. Multinational corporations (MNCs) have operations in many countries. Foreign direct investment (FDI) means a company owning facilities or businesses in another country.",
  "Firms can enter foreign markets in ways that trade off risk and control. From least to most commitment: exporting (selling abroad from home), licensing (letting a foreign firm use your brand or technology for a fee), franchising (licensing a whole business system), joint ventures (sharing ownership with a local partner) and wholly owned subsidiaries (FDI). International institutions also shape the environment: the WTO for trade rules, the International Monetary Fund (IMF) for financial stability among its 191 members, and the World Bank for development lending."],
 ["Businesses compete, sell, buy and invest globally.",
  "Multinational corporations and foreign direct investment.",
  "Entry modes: exporting, licensing, franchising, joint venture, subsidiary.",
  "More commitment = more control and more risk.",
  "Institutions: WTO, IMF, World Bank."],
 ["Global banks help multinationals manage cash, currencies and payments across countries.",
  "Investors in U.S. companies are exposed to the global economy, since many earn a large share of revenue abroad.",
  "Small businesses can start global sales with low risk through exporting, supported by trade finance and export insurance."],
 ["U.S. companies make up roughly 60% of global stock market value (MSCI All Country World Index weights).",
  "The IMF has 191 member countries and lends to countries facing financial crises (IMF).",
  "Export help: the Export-Import Bank of the United States offers export credit insurance that protects small exporters if a foreign buyer doesn't pay (EXIM)."],
 "Don't skip straight to building a factory abroad. For a small business, the safest first step is exporting or licensing, which tests demand with little investment. Match the entry mode to the client's size and risk tolerance.",
 "\"For a small business, the lowest-risk way to go global is exporting first. We'd test demand online, get paid with a letter of credit and only consider a foreign partner once sales prove out.\"",
 [("What's the safest way for this small business to start selling internationally?",
   "Start by exporting, which takes the least money and risk. The business can sell online or through a foreign distributor without opening anything abroad. To protect payment, use a letter of credit or export credit insurance from the Export-Import Bank. Pick one or two countries with strong demand and simple rules first, often Canada or Mexico under USMCA. If sales grow, the next step could be licensing or a joint venture, but only after demand is proven."),
  ("Why do investors care about the global economy?",
   "Because company profits, interest rates and currencies are all connected worldwide. Many U.S. companies earn a large share of their revenue abroad, so a slowdown in Europe or China hits their earnings. U.S. companies are also only about 60% of global stock value. For our client, that means watching global conditions and diversifying internationally, so the portfolio isn't tied to one economy.")],
 [("Multinational corporation (MNC)","A company with operations in more than one country.","Apple, Toyota, JPMorgan Chase."),
  ("Foreign direct investment (FDI)","A company owning or controlling a business or facility in another country.","The highest-commitment entry mode."),
  ("Modes of market entry","Exporting, licensing, franchising, joint venture and wholly owned subsidiary.","Ordered from least to most commitment and risk.")])

# ======================= Doing business around the world =======================
p("EC:110", "Cultural considerations in global business relations",
 ["Culture is the shared values, beliefs, customs and behaviors of a group, and it shapes how people do business. Communication styles differ: in low-context cultures (such as the U.S. and Germany), people say things directly and rely on written contracts; in high-context cultures (such as Japan, China and many Arab countries), meaning depends more on relationships, tone and what isn't said. Attitudes toward time differ: monochronic cultures value punctuality and one task at a time, while polychronic cultures are more flexible with schedules.",
  "Other differences include hierarchy and formality (titles, who speaks first), relationship building before business, decision-making speed, negotiation style, gift giving, nonverbal gestures, dress, religious holidays and meal customs. In Japan, for example, business cards are exchanged with both hands and studied respectfully. Researchers such as Geert Hofstede describe cultural dimensions like individualism versus collectivism and power distance. Respecting these differences builds trust; ignoring them can sink a deal even when the numbers work."],
 ["Low-context (direct, written) vs high-context (relationships, implied meaning).",
  "Monochronic vs polychronic time.",
  "Hierarchy, formality, relationship building and negotiation style vary.",
  "Gifts, gestures, holidays and meals carry meaning.",
  "Research the culture before the meeting."],
 ["Financial advisors serve clients from many cultures, who may have different views on debt, family money decisions and trust.",
  "Banks doing cross-border deals adjust how they negotiate and document agreements.",
  "Multilingual service and cultural awareness help firms reach new customer groups at home."],
 ["In Japan, business cards (meishi) are offered and received with both hands and read carefully before being put away; treating one casually is seen as disrespectful (JETRO business etiquette guidance).",
  "Hofstede's research found large differences in individualism: the U.S. scores among the most individualistic countries, while many Asian and Latin American countries are more collectivist (Hofstede Insights).",
  "In many cultures, family members make major financial decisions together, so an advisor may need to include parents or elders in meetings."],
 "Don't rely on stereotypes. Cultural patterns are general tendencies, not rules for every person. The best answer says you'd research the culture and then pay attention to the individual client.",
 "\"Before meeting this client's partners in Japan, we'd learn the basics: exchange business cards with both hands, expect decisions to take longer and build the relationship before talking price.\"",
 [("How would you prepare for a meeting with a client from another culture?",
   "I'd research the culture's business customs, then adapt to the person in front of me. I'd learn how formal to be, how decisions are made and what to avoid. For example, in Japan, exchanging business cards with both hands shows respect, and decisions often take longer because of group consensus. In the meeting, I'd listen more, avoid slang and confirm understanding in writing. That shows respect and builds the trust any client relationship needs."),
  ("Why might a deal fall apart even when the numbers work?",
   "Because people do business with people they trust, and cultural missteps can break that trust. In high-context cultures, pushing straight to price before building a relationship can seem rude, and a direct 'no' may never come; instead, the other side quietly walks away. So the numbers are only half the deal. We'd invest time in relationship building, use a local adviser or interpreter and adapt our communication style.")],
 [("High-context vs low-context culture","High-context: meaning relies on relationships and implied cues. Low-context: meaning is stated directly and in writing.","Japan (high) vs U.S. (low)."),
  ("Monochronic vs polychronic time","Monochronic: punctual, one task at a time. Polychronic: flexible schedules, many things at once.","Affects meeting expectations."),
  ("Power distance","How much a culture accepts unequal power and hierarchy.","High power distance = more formality with senior people.")])

p("EC:045", "Cultural and social environments and global trade",
 ["A country's cultural and social environment affects what people buy, how they buy and what businesses can sell. Religion, values, diet, language, family structure, social class, education, age of the population and attitudes toward money all shape demand. Products often need to be adapted: changed in ingredients, packaging, size, name, price or marketing message. A product or ad that works in one country can fail, or offend, in another.",
  "Social trends matter too: an aging population creates demand for health care and retirement products; a growing middle class creates demand for cars, travel and financial services; urbanization changes how people shop. In finance, beliefs shape products: Islamic finance follows Sharia principles that prohibit charging or paying interest (riba), so banks offer profit-sharing, leasing and asset-backed products such as sukuk instead of conventional interest-bearing loans and bonds."],
 ["Culture and society shape demand: religion, diet, values, family, language, age.",
  "Adapt products: ingredients, size, name, packaging, price, message.",
  "Social trends: aging populations, a growing middle class, urbanization.",
  "Islamic finance avoids interest (riba); uses profit-sharing, leasing, sukuk.",
  "Global brands often 'think global, act local.'"],
 ["Banks design products that fit cultural beliefs, such as Sharia-compliant home financing.",
  "Remittance services serve immigrant communities sending money to family abroad.",
  "Marketing financial products in other languages and cultural contexts reaches new customers."],
 ["McDonald's in India serves no beef or pork products and built its menu around chicken and vegetarian items such as the McAloo Tikki potato burger (McDonald's India).",
  "Walmart left Germany in 2006 after losses, partly because practices like greeters and mandatory smiling didn't fit local shopping culture, along with strong local competition (company reports; business press).",
  "Islamic finance: sukuk are asset-backed certificates that pay investors from profits or rent instead of interest, making them Sharia-compliant alternatives to bonds (IMF; Islamic Financial Services Board)."],
 "Don't assume a product that sells at home will sell abroad unchanged. And don't confuse 'culture' with 'language': translating an ad is only one step; values, beliefs and buying habits matter more.",
 "\"A product that works here may not fit there. McDonald's succeeds in India by dropping beef entirely, so before this business exports, we'd test how local customers' values and habits change what they want.\"",
 [("Would this product sell the same way in another country?",
   "Probably not without changes, because culture and society shape demand. Religion, diet, family size, income levels and values all affect what people buy and how they decide. McDonald's built its Indian menu around chicken and vegetarian items because many customers don't eat beef. So before exporting, we'd research the market, test a small launch and adapt the product, packaging, price or message so it fits local customers."),
  ("How could a bank serve customers whose beliefs prohibit paying interest?",
   "By offering Sharia-compliant products that share profits or use leasing instead of interest. In Islamic finance, charging or paying interest, called riba, isn't allowed, so a home purchase can be structured as the bank buying the home and selling or leasing it to the customer for a set profit. On the savings side, accounts can share profits. Offering these products respectfully could open a new customer group and build loyalty in that community.")],
 [("Product adaptation","Changing a product's ingredients, size, name, packaging, price or message to fit a foreign market.","McDonald's India menu."),
  ("Islamic finance","Financial services following Sharia principles, which prohibit paying or charging interest (riba).","Uses profit-sharing, leasing and sukuk."),
  ("Social environment","Population traits and trends such as age, family structure, income and education that shape demand.","Aging populations boost retirement products.")])

p("EC:111", "Electronic communication tools in global business",
 ["Electronic communication tools such as email, video conferencing, webcasts and webinars, instant messaging, collaboration platforms and shared online documents let businesses work across borders in real time. They cut travel costs, speed up decisions, let global teams work together and help small businesses reach customers anywhere. Banks and markets depend on secure electronic networks to move money and information around the world.",
  "These tools bring challenges too: time-zone differences, misunderstandings when tone and body language are lost, information overload, and cybersecurity risks like phishing and business email compromise (BEC), where criminals impersonate executives or suppliers to trick employees into wiring money. In finance, there are also legal rules: firms must keep records of business communications, so using unapproved texting apps can break securities laws."],
 ["Tools: email, video conferencing, webcasts, messaging, collaboration platforms.",
  "Benefits: speed, lower travel costs, global teams, reach.",
  "Challenges: time zones, lost tone, overload, cybersecurity.",
  "Business email compromise (BEC) is a major fraud risk.",
  "Financial firms must preserve business communications."],
 ["Global payments run on secure messaging networks such as SWIFT.",
  "Advisors use video meetings and secure portals to serve clients anywhere.",
  "Recordkeeping rules require approved, archived channels for client communications."],
 ["The SWIFT network connects more than 11,500 financial institutions in over 200 countries and territories for secure payment messages (SWIFT).",
  "Business email compromise cost U.S. victims about $2.8 billion in 2024, and total reported internet crime losses were about $16.6 billion (FBI Internet Crime Complaint Center, 2024 report).",
  "Since 2021, the SEC and CFTC have fined Wall Street firms more than $2 billion combined for employees using unapproved texting apps like WhatsApp for business (SEC; CFTC enforcement announcements)."],
 "Don't present electronic tools as purely positive. A strong answer names a benefit and a risk, such as video meetings saving travel time but email fraud requiring call-back verification for any payment change.",
 "\"Video meetings let our client serve customers in three time zones, but email fraud is a real risk: BEC scams cost about $2.8 billion in 2024. We'd require a phone call to confirm any change to payment instructions.\"",
 [("Can our advisors text clients on WhatsApp?",
   "Only through approved channels that the firm can archive, because financial firms must keep records of business communications. Since 2021, the SEC and CFTC have fined firms more than $2 billion for employees using unapproved apps like WhatsApp. Clients like texting, so we'd offer it through a compliant platform that records messages automatically. That gives clients the convenience they want while protecting them and the firm."),
  ("How can a small business manage clients in other time zones?",
   "Use electronic tools to work asynchronously, and set clear response expectations. Online scheduling lets clients book times that work for both sides, email and shared documents keep projects moving overnight, and short video calls replace expensive trips. We'd also add security: verify any payment change by phone, since business email compromise cost U.S. victims about $2.8 billion in 2024. That lets the business serve global clients efficiently and safely.")],
 [("Business email compromise (BEC)","A scam where criminals impersonate executives or suppliers by email to trick employees into sending money.","About $2.8 billion in U.S. losses in 2024 (FBI)."),
  ("Webcast / webinar","A live or recorded online presentation viewed over the internet.","Lets firms educate many clients at once."),
  ("SWIFT","A secure messaging network banks use to send international payment instructions.","More than 11,500 institutions in 200+ countries.")])

p("EC:114", "The impact of geography on world trade",
 ["Geography, a country's location, climate, natural resources, terrain and access to water, shapes what it produces and how it trades. Countries rich in oil, minerals or farmland tend to export those resources. Coastlines, ports and navigable rivers make trade cheaper; landlocked or mountainous countries face higher transport costs. Climate decides what crops can grow and when, and natural disasters can interrupt production. Distance and time zones affect shipping times and communication.",
  "A few narrow waterways, called chokepoints, carry a huge share of world trade: the Strait of Hormuz, the Suez Canal and Red Sea, the Panama Canal and the Strait of Malacca. When one is disrupted by conflict, drought or an accident, shipping costs rise, deliveries slow and prices jump worldwide."],
 ["Geography: location, climate, resources, terrain, waterways.",
  "Natural resources shape what countries export.",
  "Ports and rivers lower transport costs; landlocked countries pay more.",
  "Chokepoints: Hormuz, Suez/Red Sea, Panama, Malacca.",
  "Disruptions raise shipping costs and prices."],
 ["Commodity prices, like oil, react to geographic risks, which affects inflation, interest rates and investments.",
  "Lenders consider location risks (floods, hurricanes) when financing property and businesses, and insurers price them.",
  "Supply-chain disruptions affect businesses' cash flow and ability to repay loans."],
 ["About one-fifth of the world's oil consumption passes through the Strait of Hormuz (U.S. Energy Information Administration). Supply disruptions there in 2026 pushed U.S. gasoline above $4.50 a gallon in the spring (BLS; University of Michigan, 2026).",
  "A severe drought in 2023 lowered water levels at the Panama Canal, forcing authorities to cut the number of ships allowed through each day (Panama Canal Authority).",
  "Attacks on ships in the Red Sea in 2023-24 led many shipping lines to reroute around Africa's Cape of Good Hope, adding about 10 days to Asia-Europe voyages (shipping industry reports)."],
 "Don't treat geography as only 'where a country is on the map.' Judges want you to connect it to costs and risks: shipping routes, natural resources, climate risk and chokepoints.",
 "\"Geography matters for this importer: its goods ship through the Red Sea. When shipping lines rerouted around Africa, voyages took about 10 days longer, so we'd hold extra inventory and quote customers longer lead times.\"",
 [("How could geography affect this business's supply chain?",
   "Through shipping routes, distance and natural risks. If this business imports from Asia, its goods may pass chokepoints like the Red Sea or the Strait of Malacca, where disruptions add time and cost. In 2023-24, rerouting around Africa added about 10 days to Asia-Europe trips. Suppliers in hurricane or flood zones add risk too. So we'd map where each key part comes from, keep a few extra weeks of inventory and consider a closer supplier, such as one in Mexico."),
  ("Why did gas prices jump when shipping in the Middle East was disrupted?",
   "Because a large share of the world's oil travels through one narrow waterway. About one-fifth of global oil consumption passes through the Strait of Hormuz, so when shipping there was disrupted in 2026, oil supply tightened and prices rose; U.S. gasoline topped $4.50 a gallon in the spring. That's supply and demand driven by geography. For our client, it shows why an emergency fund and a flexible budget help when energy prices spike.")],
 [("Chokepoint","A narrow route, like a strait or canal, that a large share of trade must pass through.","Strait of Hormuz, Suez Canal, Panama Canal."),
  ("Landlocked country","A country with no coastline, which usually faces higher transport costs for trade.","Must ship through neighbors' ports."),
  ("Geographic risk","Risk to trade from location, climate and natural events.","Drought cut Panama Canal transits in 2023.")])

p("EC:115", "A country's history and world trade",
 ["A country's history shapes its trade today. Past colonial ties often leave shared languages, legal systems and business connections; many former British colonies use common law and English in business, which makes trade with English-speaking countries easier. Past wars and conflicts can leave lasting distrust or sanctions. Historic trade routes and long-standing partnerships continue for generations.",
  "Economic history matters too. Countries that opened their economies changed their trade dramatically: China began market reforms in 1978 and joined the WTO in 2001, becoming the world's largest goods exporter; India liberalized its economy in 1991. Former Soviet-bloc countries moved from command toward market economies after 1991. Experiences like hyperinflation or currency crises shape how people save and invest for decades: Germany's hyperinflation in 1923 is one reason its central bank traditions focus heavily on fighting inflation."],
 ["Colonial ties: shared language, legal systems and business networks.",
  "Conflicts can leave distrust or sanctions.",
  "Economic reforms reshape trade (China 1978, WTO 2001; India 1991).",
  "History of inflation or crises shapes savings habits.",
  "Long-standing partnerships continue for generations."],
 ["Clients' histories shape their money behavior: people who lived through bank failures or inflation abroad may distrust banks or prefer cash or gold.",
  "Countries with a history of currency crises often see residents save in U.S. dollars.",
  "Shared legal traditions make contracts and investment easier between some countries."],
 ["China joined the World Trade Organization in 2001, after market reforms that began in 1978, and became the world's largest exporter of goods (WTO).",
  "Germany's hyperinflation of 1923, when prices doubled every few days at its peak, shaped a lasting national focus on price stability (Deutsche Bundesbank history).",
  "Hong Kong, a former British colony, still uses a common-law legal system, which helped make it a major international finance center (Hong Kong Department of Justice)."],
 "Don't just list historical facts. Connect history to today's trade: why a language, a legal system or a memory of crisis changes how business is done now.",
 "\"History explains this client's caution. If their family lived through a currency collapse, keeping cash at home makes sense to them. We'd start by showing them how FDIC insurance protects deposits here.\"",
 [("Why do some countries trade more with each other?",
   "Often because of shared history: language, legal systems and long-standing relationships make trade easier and cheaper. Former British colonies, for example, often share English and common law, which simplifies contracts. Historic partnerships build trust that lasts generations. Geography and trade agreements matter too. For this business, it means the easiest export markets may be the ones with shared language and similar laws, such as Canada or the U.K."),
  ("How could a country's past affect how its people save and invest?",
   "Experiences like hyperinflation, bank failures or currency collapses teach people to distrust banks or the local currency, and that memory lasts for decades. Germany's 1923 hyperinflation made fighting inflation a national priority. A client whose family lived through a crisis abroad may keep cash at home or prefer gold. As advisors, we'd respect that history, explain FDIC insurance and start with simple, safe steps, like an insured high-yield savings account, to build trust.")],
 [("Colonial legacy in trade","Shared language, legal systems and business ties left from colonial history.","Common law in many former British colonies."),
  ("China's WTO entry (2001)","China joined the World Trade Organization after reforms beginning in 1978, becoming the largest goods exporter.","Economic history reshaping trade."),
  ("Hyperinflation","Extremely rapid, out-of-control inflation, such as in Germany in 1923.","Shapes saving habits for generations.")])

p("EC:116", "A country's economic development and world trade",
 ["Countries are at different stages of economic development, and that affects what they trade and how. Developed (advanced) economies such as the U.S., Japan and Germany have high incomes, strong infrastructure, educated workers and service-heavy economies. Developing economies have lower incomes and often rely more on agriculture or raw materials. Emerging markets, such as India, Brazil and Mexico, are growing quickly and becoming more industrial. The World Bank classifies countries by income per person as low, lower-middle, upper-middle or high income.",
  "Development level shapes trade: developing countries often export raw materials and import manufactured goods; as they develop, they export more manufactured goods and services. Less developed countries may have weaker infrastructure, less access to banking, less stable currencies and higher risk, but also faster growth and young populations. Investors see emerging markets as higher risk with higher potential growth."],
 ["Developed, developing and emerging economies.",
  "World Bank income groups: low, lower-middle, upper-middle, high.",
  "Development affects exports, infrastructure, banking access and risk.",
  "Emerging markets: faster growth, higher risk.",
  "Financial inclusion grows as economies develop."],
 ["Emerging-market funds offer growth potential but more volatility and currency and political risk.",
  "Banking products in developing countries often run on mobile phones, since many people skipped traditional branches.",
  "Development banks like the World Bank lend to support infrastructure and growth."],
 ["Worldwide, 79% of adults had an account at a bank, other financial institution or mobile money provider in 2024, up from 51% in 2011 (World Bank Global Findex 2025).",
  "Kenya's M-Pesa, launched in 2007, let millions of people send and store money by mobile phone without a bank account (Safaricom; World Bank).",
  "India became the world's most populous country in 2023 and is one of the fastest-growing major economies (United Nations; IMF World Economic Outlook)."],
 "Don't call every non-U.S. economy 'developing.' Countries like Japan and Germany are developed, and emerging markets like India and Brazil are a distinct middle group. Use the right term.",
 "\"Emerging markets like India are growing fast, but they bring more ups and downs. For our client, a small slice through a diversified emerging-markets fund captures that growth without betting on one country.\"",
 [("Should our client invest in emerging markets?",
   "A small, diversified amount can make sense for a long-term investor. Emerging markets like India and Brazil often grow faster than developed economies, with young populations and rising middle classes. But they carry more risk: political instability, weaker currencies and bigger price swings. So we'd use a low-cost emerging-markets index fund for a modest slice of the stock portfolio, perhaps 5% to 10%, and hold it for at least ten years, so our client gets the growth potential without too much risk."),
  ("How would you adapt a banking product for a developing country?",
   "Build it around mobile phones, low costs and small balances, because many customers have phones but limited access to branches. Kenya's M-Pesa showed that people will save and send money by phone without a traditional bank account. Global Findex data show account ownership reached 79% of adults in 2024, largely thanks to mobile money. So we'd offer a phone-based account with no minimum balance, tiny fees and simple savings goals, then add small loans once customers build a history.")],
 [("Emerging market","A fast-growing economy moving from developing toward developed, such as India, Brazil or Mexico.","Higher growth potential, higher risk."),
  ("World Bank income groups","Countries classified by income per person: low, lower-middle, upper-middle and high income.","Used to compare development."),
  ("Financial inclusion","Access to useful, affordable financial services such as accounts, payments, savings and credit.","79% of adults had an account in 2024 (Global Findex).")])

p("EC:140", "Bribery and foreign payments",
 ["Bribery is offering, giving or accepting something of value to improperly influence a decision, such as paying a government official to win a contract or a license. Beyond being unethical, it is illegal for U.S. businesses abroad. The Foreign Corrupt Practices Act (FCPA) of 1977 bans U.S. companies, citizens and companies listed on U.S. stock exchanges from bribing foreign officials, and it requires public companies to keep accurate books and internal controls so bribes can't be hidden. The U.K. Bribery Act of 2010 is even stricter and also covers private-sector bribery. Dozens of countries have signed the OECD Anti-Bribery Convention.",
  "The FCPA has a narrow exception for small 'facilitating payments' for routine government actions, like processing paperwork, but the U.K. law doesn't allow them, and many companies ban them entirely. Bribery hurts business: it raises costs, distorts competition, invites blackmail, risks huge fines and prison, and destroys reputations. Companies fight it with codes of conduct, training, due diligence on agents and partners, and hotlines."],
 ["Bribery: giving something of value to improperly influence a decision.",
  "FCPA (1977): anti-bribery plus accurate books and internal controls.",
  "U.K. Bribery Act (2010): stricter, covers private bribery, no facilitation payments.",
  "OECD Anti-Bribery Convention.",
  "Prevention: codes of conduct, training, due diligence, hotlines."],
 ["Banks must screen for corruption risk and report suspicious transactions under anti-money-laundering rules.",
  "Investors lose when companies are caught: fines, lost contracts and stock price drops.",
  "Advisors and bankers must refuse gifts or payments meant to influence their decisions."],
 ["Siemens paid about $1.6 billion to U.S. and German authorities in 2008 to settle bribery charges, including $800 million in the U.S., one of the largest FCPA cases ever (U.S. Department of Justice; SEC, 2008).",
  "Goldman Sachs agreed in 2020 to pay more than $2.9 billion to resolve U.S. charges over bribes paid to officials in Malaysia and Abu Dhabi in connection with the 1MDB fund (U.S. Department of Justice, 2020).",
  "In 2025, a February executive order paused new FCPA enforcement for review, and in June 2025 the Justice Department issued new guidelines and resumed cases focused on serious misconduct; the law itself did not change (U.S. Department of Justice, June 9, 2025)."],
 "Don't say a bribe is fine if it's 'how business is done there.' U.S. law applies to U.S. companies anywhere in the world. And don't confuse a small, legal facilitating payment with a bribe to win business; when in doubt, refuse and ask legal.",
 "\"We won't pay it. Under the FCPA, paying a foreign official to win business is a federal crime, even overseas. We'd report the request to our compliance team and look for a legitimate way to speed up the license.\"",
 [("A foreign official hints that a 'fee' will speed up our license. What do you do?",
   "I'd politely decline, document the request and report it to our compliance or legal team right away. Paying a foreign official to win or keep business is illegal under the FCPA, even if it's common in that country, and the U.K. Bribery Act bans even small facilitating payments. Siemens paid about $1.6 billion in 2008 to settle bribery charges. So we'd pursue the license through official channels and, if needed, get help from the U.S. embassy's commercial service."),
  ("Why does bribery hurt business even if it gets deals done?",
   "Because the short-term win creates huge long-term risks and costs. Bribery is illegal under the FCPA, with fines in the billions and possible prison; Goldman Sachs paid over $2.9 billion over the 1MDB bribes. It also invites more demands, since officials who've been paid once expect it again, and it destroys trust with customers and investors. Companies that compete honestly build reputations that win business over time.")],
 [("Foreign Corrupt Practices Act (FCPA)","A 1977 U.S. law banning bribery of foreign officials and requiring public companies to keep accurate books and internal controls.","Applies to U.S. companies anywhere in the world."),
  ("Facilitating payment","A small payment to speed up a routine government action; narrowly allowed under the FCPA, banned under the U.K. Bribery Act.","Many companies ban them entirely."),
  ("U.K. Bribery Act (2010)","A strict anti-bribery law covering public and private bribery and banning facilitating payments.","Stricter than the FCPA.")])

p("EC:141", "Requirements for international business travel",
 ["Traveling abroad for business takes planning. A passport is required for international air travel, and many countries require it to be valid for at least six months beyond your trip and to have blank pages. A visa is official permission to enter a country for a purpose, such as tourism or business; some countries require one in advance, others allow visa-free entry for short visits, and some use electronic travel authorizations. Business visas may require a sponsorship or invitation letter from the company you're visiting, explaining the purpose and length of the trip.",
  "Travelers may need proof of citizenship, proof of a return ticket or proof of funds. Some countries require certain immunizations: for example, proof of yellow fever vaccination for some countries in Africa and South America. Smart travelers also check State Department travel advisories, enroll in the Smart Traveler Enrollment Program (STEP), buy travel and medical insurance, notify their bank, and bring cards that don't charge foreign transaction fees. Inside the U.S., a REAL ID-compliant license or passport has been required for domestic flights since May 7, 2025."],
 ["Passport: often must be valid six months beyond the trip.",
  "Visa: permission to enter for a purpose; business visas may need a sponsorship letter.",
  "Proof of citizenship, return ticket or funds may be required.",
  "Immunizations: some countries require proof (yellow fever).",
  "Check travel.state.gov advisories, enroll in STEP, get travel insurance."],
 ["Business travelers should tell their bank and card issuers where they'll be to avoid declined cards.",
  "Cards without foreign transaction fees save money (many cards charge about 3%).",
  "Companies buy travel and medical insurance for employees, since U.S. health plans often don't cover care abroad."],
 ["The State Department's travel.state.gov lists each country's entry requirements, including passport validity, visas and vaccinations, plus travel advisories (U.S. Department of State).",
  "The CDC lists which countries require proof of yellow fever vaccination, and certificates must be issued by authorized clinics (CDC Travelers' Health).",
  "REAL ID enforcement began May 7, 2025: domestic flyers need a REAL ID-compliant license or another accepted ID such as a passport (Transportation Security Administration)."],
 "Don't assume a valid passport is enough. Many countries also require a visa, six months of remaining passport validity, or proof of vaccination, and rules change. Always check the official State Department country page before booking.",
 "\"Before this trip, our client should check the State Department's country page: passport valid six months past the return date, the right business visa with a sponsorship letter, and any required vaccinations.\"",
 [("Our client is traveling to meet a supplier overseas next month. What should they check?",
   "Start with the State Department's country page on travel.state.gov. First, the passport: it should be valid at least six months past the return date with blank pages. Second, the visa: does the country require a business visa, and does it need a sponsorship letter from the supplier? Third, health: any required vaccinations, like yellow fever, and travel medical insurance. Finally, money: tell the bank and bring a card with no foreign transaction fees. A month is tight for some visas, so they should apply this week."),
  ("Why would a company pay for an employee's travel insurance?",
   "Because U.S. health insurance often doesn't cover care abroad, and an emergency overseas can cost thousands of dollars, or far more for a medical evacuation. Travel insurance also covers trip cancellations, lost bags and delays. For the company, it protects the employee, which is part of its duty of care, and limits a costly surprise. It's a small cost that transfers a large risk, which is classic risk management.")],
 [("Visa","Official permission from a country to enter for a specific purpose and length of stay.","Business visas may require a sponsorship letter."),
  ("Passport six-month rule","Many countries require a passport to be valid at least six months beyond the trip.","Check travel.state.gov."),
  ("Smart Traveler Enrollment Program (STEP)","A free State Department program that registers U.S. travelers with the nearest embassy for alerts and emergencies.","Recommended for business travel.")])
